# teademo: Tea program + TextInput widget under a real pty.
# Frame trace (DIFFERENTIAL repaints: each frame is ONE write; first paint
# hides the cursor; repaints address rows ABSOLUTELY — a changed row
# rewrites as \e[row;1H + \e[2K + text with NO trailing \r\n, an unchanged
# row emits NOTHING):
#   init    \e[?25l \e[2K\e[7m \e[0m\r\n
#   'h'     \e[1;1H\e[2Kh\e[7m \e[0m
#   'i'     \e[1;1H\e[2Khi\e[7m \e[0m
#   \e[A    (NOTHING: ignored key -> UNCHANGED model -> runtime-owned
#            skip-render drops the whole repaint — the frame would have been
#            byte-identical, and even a repaint of an identical frame emits
#            zero bytes: unchanged rows cost nothing.)
#   0x7F    \e[1;1H\e[2Kh\e[7m \e[0m
#   \r      \e[?25h + exit 0
# The single expect below spans the 'i' frame's tail DIRECTLY into the
# backspace frame's head (the 'i' frame no longer ends in \r\n — absolute
# addressing leaves the cursor where the rewrite put it): such a substring
# can only exist when the up-arrow wrote NOTHING between them — under a
# no-op-key repaint of the old relative renderer the \e[1A\r\n churn would
# sit in between and the expect would time out.  So this asserts BOTH halves
# of skip-render: the no-op key emits no repaint bytes, and the changing
# update (backspace) still paints.
expect \e[?25l
send hi
expect hi\e[7m
send \e[A
send \x7f
expect hi\e[7m \e[0m\e[1;1H\e[2Kh\e[7m
send \r
expect \e[?25h
expect_exit 0
